  "title": "Set Notation",
  "body": " Set Notation       Strand  1.0 Numbers and Algebra    Sub-Strand  1.1 Sets    Specific Learning Outcomes  Identify set notations and elements of a set in varied situations    Grade Level  12    Estimated Duration  40 minutes           How do sets help us sort, describe and count the things around us?   Every day we sort things into groups. A school sorts its learners into classes, clubs and games; a county agricultural officer counts the farmers who grow maize, the farmers who grow beans, and the farmers who grow both; an M-Pesa agent records which customers also have a bank account.  A set describes such a group exactly, so that everyone agrees on what belongs to it. In this chapter you will learn to write sets, to describe them in different ways, to combine them, to draw them in Venn diagrams, and to use them to count without counting anyone twice. Keep the key inquiry question in mind as you work.   Set Symbols in This Book    : curly brackets (braces) hold the elements of a set, separated by commas, for example .  : is an element of .  : is not an element of .  : the number of elements in .    Later sections add more symbols to this list, each where it is first needed.    Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: a set, an element, the symbols , and , and what makes a set well defined.  Do: list the elements of a described set, decide whether an object belongs to a set, and count the elements.  Apply: sorting classroom objects; the letters of the names of Kenyan counties.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners sort desk items into labelled groups and write each group in curly brackets.  Teacher's Role Put one group's items on the front desk. Before naming the idea of a well-defined set, ask: Could two people disagree about what belongs in this group?     Sorting the Classroom   On the front desk are a ruler, a pen, a pencil, an eraser, an exercise book, a textbook, a bottle top, a piece of chalk and a sharpener.     List , the set of things on the desk that are used for writing.  .    Fill in or : chalk ; ruler .  chalk ; ruler .    Find .  .    Write a rule for the set .  For example .    Why is not a set?  There is no clear test for belonging: one learner may think a bottle top is useful and another may not.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise sets, elements and the set symbols from the activity.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (e) shows why a set needs a clear rule; parts (a) to (c) used braces, , and .  Write the elements in curly brackets, separated by commas, each element once.  means is an element of , means is not an element of , and is the number of elements.      Sets and Elements  A set is a collection with a clear rule for what belongs. The objects in a set are its elements (or members). We name sets with capital letters and write the elements in curly brackets, separated by commas: .    means is an element of ; means is not an element of .  is the number of elements in ; here .  Order does not matter and each element is written once: and are the same set.  A set must be well defined : for any object, there is a clear answer to Does it belong?      Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Letters of a County   List the set and find .    KIRINYAGA has nine letters, but I appears twice and A appears twice. Each element is written once, so and , not .     Factors and Membership   List . Is ? Is ? Find .    The factors of come in pairs: , , , . So . Then , and .     Well Defined or Not?   Which of these are sets? List any that are.           (a) Not a set: tall has no clear test, so two people could disagree. (b) A set: a tape measure decides who belongs. (c) A set: , with .     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type a set inside curly brackets with commas between the elements, for example {2, 5, 9} . On most phone keyboards the curly brackets are on the second page of symbols: tap ?123 (or 123 ), then =\\< (or #+= ). Type the empty set as {} . Type a count as a whole number.            List the elements of and find the number of elements.  ; elements    List and find the number of elements.  ; elements     True or false?          (a) True. (b) False. (c) False: the set is , so .    Describe the set in words.  The prime numbers less than (or less than : both are correct).    List the set of two-digit numbers whose tens digit is twice the units digit.   The units digit can be , , or (a units digit of would need a tens digit of , and a units digit of gives the one-digit number ). So the numbers are , , and .    Challenge: Zero and Nothing  Explain why and are different sets, and give the number of elements of each.  has one element, the number , so . has no elements, so .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Work through the activity, and . Give a card: braces; is in; is not in; = how many . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: write a set of four Kenyan counties whose names have exactly five letters (for example ; the teacher checks).      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
