  "url": "subsec-two-set-venn-diagrams.html",
  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "1.5.1",
  "title": "Venn Diagrams with Two Sets",
  "body": " Venn Diagrams with Two Sets  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: the parts of a Venn diagram, the four regions and their numbers, and .  Do: place elements in a diagram, describe shaded regions, and count.  Apply: subject choices in a class.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners place numbers in a two-set Venn diagram and discover the rule for counting a union.  Teacher's Role Draw the blank diagram on the board. Ask: Which numbers go in the overlap? before any are placed.     Multiples of Two and Three   , is the set of multiples of in and is the set of multiples of in .   A blank Venn diagram for the sets and .   A rectangle for the universal set with two overlapping circles labelled T and H. No numbers are written in yet.        Copy the diagram and write each number from to in the region where it belongs.   Both: . only: . only: . Outside both: .   The numbers to placed in the diagram.   The Venn diagram for T and H with the numbers placed. T only: 2, 4, 8, 10, 14. Both: 6, 12. H only: 3, 9, 15. Outside both: 1, 5, 7, 11, 13.        Find , , and .  , , and .    Find a rule that links the four numbers in part (b).  : adding and counts and twice, so subtract once.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To name the regions of a two-set diagram and state the counting rule.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (c) is the rule .  Fill the overlap first, then the rest of each circle, then the outside.  A shaded set can be described by its region numbers.      Two-Set Venn Diagrams  In a Venn diagram the universal set is a rectangle and each set is a circle, labelled outside the circle. Elements, or counts of elements, are written in the regions. In this book the four regions of a two-set diagram are numbered as in : region is in only, region is in both, region is in only and region is outside both. So is regions , is and is .  For counting, fill the region that belongs to the most sets first, then work outwards. Adding and counts region twice, so    The four regions of a two-set Venn diagram.   A rectangle for the universal set with two overlapping circles A and B. Region 1 is A only, region 2 is both, region 3 is B only, region 4 is outside both.      Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Complements by Region   Give the region numbers of , , and .    and . , so . . These are two ways to describe the outside of both circles.     Agriculture and Computer Studies   In a class of learners, take Agriculture, take Computer Studies and take both. How many take Agriculture only, Computer Studies only, and neither? Find by the formula.    Fill the overlap first: . Agriculture only ; Computer Studies only ; neither . By the formula, .   Agriculture and Computer Studies in a class of .   A two-set Venn diagram, Agriculture and Computer Studies. Agriculture only 19, both 9, Computer Studies only 13, neither 4.  Region counts: Agriculture only ; both ; Computer Studies only ; neither . Total .        Finding the Overlap   In a youth group of , have a smartphone, have a bicycle and have neither. How many have both?    . Then .     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type a set inside curly brackets with commas between the elements, for example {2, 5, 9} . On most phone keyboards the curly brackets are on the second page of symbols: tap ?123 (or 123 ), then =\\< (or #+= ). Type the empty set as {} . Type a count as a whole number.        , is the set of multiples of and is the set of odd numbers in . Draw the Venn diagram. List and the elements outside both circles.  only ; ; only ; outside both .    Using the region numbers of , give the regions of (a) (b) (c) .  (a) (b) (c)    Of learners, play a sport, sing in the choir and do neither. How many do both? How many play a sport only? How many sing in the choir only?  Both ; sport only ; choir only  , so both . Sport only and choir only .    , and . Find .     , and . Find .     Challenge: Must They Meet?  , and . Can and have no element in common?  No. , so at least elements are in both.     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Work through the activity, and . Give a card with . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including .      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
