  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "2.1.1",
  "title": "Sharing in a Ratio",
  "body": " Sharing in a Ratio  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: a ratio, its parts, and a share.  Do: share a quantity in a two-part or three-part ratio; find the total or the other shares from one share or from the difference between two shares.  Apply: sharing money, stationery and farm inputs.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners share real counters in given ratios and discover that the key step is to find one part first.  Teacher's Role Give each group of three bottle tops (or beans, or sweets). Before any rule, ask: How many bottle tops is one part?     Sharing Bottle Tops   Work in groups of three. Your group has bottle tops.     Share the bottle tops in the ratio . How many does each of you get?  There are parts, so one part is . The shares are , and .    Share them again in the ratio .  parts of : , and .    Share them in the ratio .  parts of : , and .    Try to share them in the ratio . What goes wrong?  There are parts, and is not a whole number, so some bottle tops are left over (or would have to be cut).    Is sharing equally always fair? When would sharing in a ratio be fairer?  Open. Sharing in a ratio is fairer when people did different amounts of work or put in different amounts of money.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise the sharing method from the activity.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: in every part of the activity the groups found one part first: divided by the total number of parts.  Each share is its number of parts times one part.  The shares must add back to the total; this is the check.      Sharing in a Ratio  A ratio such as compares quantities. Its numbers are parts , and the amount each person gets is a share . To share a total in the ratio :  Total number of parts: .  One part: total .  Each share: its number of parts one part.  Check: the shares add up to the total.  So the first person gets of the total. Find one part first, then each share is a number of parts.    Sharing bottle tops in the ratio with a bar model.   Three bars of 5, 7 and 8 equal boxes, each box marked 3, make 20 parts. 60 bottle tops make each part 3, so the shares are 15, 21 and 24.      Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Stationery for Three Classes   A school shares exercise books among classes 12A, 12B and 12C in the ratio . How many does each class get?    Total parts: . One part: books.  12A gets , 12B gets and 12C gets exercise books.  Check: .     Shares from One Share   Achieng, Baraka and Chebet share the profit from their poultry project in the ratio . Chebet gets Ksh . Find the total profit and the other two shares.    Chebet's parts are Ksh , so one part is .  There are parts, so the total profit is , Ksh . Achieng gets and Baraka : Ksh and Ksh .     Shares from a Difference   Two dairy co-operatives share their income from a milk cooler in the ratio . The first gets Ksh more than the second. Find each share and the total.    The difference is parts, so parts are Ksh and one part is .  The first gets and the second : Ksh and Ksh , Ksh in all.     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type numbers only, without Ksh, units, spaces or commas, for example 45326 . Give a fraction as a fraction, for example 7\/12 , or as a decimal correct to 3 decimal places. Give times, masses and volumes exactly or correct to 1 decimal place, and money to the nearest shilling, unless the question says otherwise.        Share Ksh in the ratio .  Ksh and Ksh    Divide of fertiliser among three farmers in the ratio .  , and  parts, and one part is . The shares are , and .    A matatu SACCO shares Ksh among three drivers, Otieno, Kamau and Wekesa, in the ratio of the days they worked, . How much does each driver get?  Otieno Ksh ; Kamau and Wekesa Ksh each    Mangoes are sorted into grades A, B and C in the ratio . There are grade A mangoes. How many mangoes are there altogether, and how many are grade C?  mangoes; grade C  parts are , so one part is . There are parts, mangoes, and grade C is .    Money is shared in the ratio . The larger share is Ksh more than the smaller. Find the total.  Ksh  The difference is parts, so one part is . The total is .    Challenge: Which Totals Share Exactly?  Can bottle tops be shared exactly in the ratio ? Which totals from to can be?  How many parts are there? Which totals divide exactly by that number?  No; and  There are parts, and is not a multiple of . The multiples of from to are and .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Activity parts (a) to (c) with real counters, and . Give a bar-model card like . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: share Ksh in the ratio . What fraction of the total does each person get? (Answer: Ksh , , and ; , , and .)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
