  "url": "subsec-proportional-parts-in-practice.html",
  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "2.1.2",
  "title": "Proportional Parts in Practice",
  "body": " Proportional Parts in Practice  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: sharing in proportion to amounts; a ratio of fractions; increasing or decreasing in a ratio.  Do: find a ratio from contributions, days worked or fractions, then share; change a quantity in a given ratio; work back to a total.  Apply: chamas, farmers, workers' pay and fair shares.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners compare equal sharing with sharing in proportion to what each person put in, and argue which is fair.  Teacher's Role Give each group member the view of one chama member for part (d); let each group report its decision and its reasons.     Fair Shares in a Chama   Three members of a chama put Ksh , Ksh and Ksh into a posho mill. This month the mill made a profit of Ksh .     Write the ratio of their contributions in its simplest form.  Divide each by : .    Share the profit in this ratio.  parts of Ksh : Ksh , Ksh and Ksh .    How much would each get if the profit were shared equally?  , so about Ksh each (the shillings do not divide exactly: in practice Ksh , and ).    Who gains and who loses by sharing equally? Which way is fairer here, and why?  Compared with sharing in proportion, the first member gains about Ksh and the second about Ksh , and the third loses about Ksh (exactly, the gains equal the loss ). Sharing in proportion is fairer, because the members risked their money in that ratio.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise sharing in proportion, ratios of fractions, and changing a quantity in a given ratio.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (a) turned the contributions into a ratio before sharing: in proportion to means in the ratio of .  A ratio of fractions is cleared by multiplying every fraction by the lowest common multiple of the denominators.  To increase in the ratio , multiply by ; to decrease in the ratio , multiply by .      Proportional Parts  To share in proportion to some amounts, share in the ratio of those amounts (simplify the ratio first if you like). Contributions of Ksh , and give the ratio .  A ratio of fractions such as becomes a ratio of whole numbers when every fraction is multiplied by the lowest common multiple of the denominators, here : .  To increase in the ratio  or decrease in the ratio  , multiply by the first number over the second: new old , or old .    Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       A Ratio Made of Fractions   Share Ksh in the ratio .    The lowest common multiple of , and is . Multiplying each fraction by gives , which is parts.  One part is , so the shares are Ksh , Ksh and Ksh .     Increase and Decrease in a Ratio   (a) A jua kali workshop makes jiko a week. Its output increases in the ratio . How many does it make now?  (b) A bus fare of Ksh decreases in the ratio . What is the new fare?    (a) jiko a week.  (b) , so Ksh .     Sharing a Repair Bill   Three neighbours keep , and cows that drink from a shared borehole. They share a repair bill of Ksh in proportion to the number of cows. How much does each pay?    The ratio simplifies to , which is parts. One part is .  They pay Ksh , Ksh and Ksh .     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type numbers only, without Ksh, units, spaces or commas, for example 45326 . Give a fraction as a fraction, for example 7\/12 , or as a decimal correct to 3 decimal places. Give times, masses and volumes exactly or correct to 1 decimal place, and money to the nearest shilling, unless the question says otherwise.            Share Ksh in the ratio .  ; Ksh , Ksh and Ksh  Multiply by : , parts. One part is .    (a) Increase in the ratio . (b) Decrease Ksh in the ratio .  (a) (b) Ksh    Three schools paid Ksh , Ksh and Ksh for a joint trip to Hell's Gate. A refund of Ksh is shared in proportion to what they paid. How much does each school get back?  ; Ksh , Ksh and Ksh    The price of a phone rose in the ratio to Ksh . What was the old price?  Ksh  New old , so old .    Three casual workers worked , and days on a building site. They share Ksh in proportion to the days they worked. How much does each get?  ; Ksh , Ksh and Ksh    Challenge: Combining Two Ratios  and . Find , then share Ksh in this ratio.  Make the two values of the same: what is the lowest common multiple of and ?  ; Ksh , Ksh and Ksh  Make equal to in both: and , so . That is parts, and one part is .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Activity parts (a) and (b), and . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: design a fair way to share a group's profit when members gave both money and days of work.      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
