  "id": "subsec-mixtures-in-a-ratio",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "subsec-mixtures-in-a-ratio.html",
  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "2.4.1",
  "title": "Mixtures in a Ratio",
  "body": " Mixtures in a Ratio  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: the components of a mixture made in a ratio; the cost of 1 kg or 1 litre of a mixture.  Do: find the components; find the cost per kilogram or per litre; explain why the mean price is usually wrong.  Apply: githeri, chai, uji flour, animal feed and fertiliser.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners mix and cost maize and beans at local prices and find when the mean of the two prices gives the right cost.  Teacher's Role If learners know today's prices at the local market, use them; otherwise use the prices in the activity.     Costing Githeri   Maize costs Ksh a kilogram and beans Ksh a kilogram.     Mix of maize with of beans. What does the mix cost, and what does 1 kg of it cost?  , so Ksh for , and , Ksh a kilogram.    Mix maize and beans in the ratio . What does 1 kg cost?  cost , and , so about Ksh a kilogram.    Mix them in the ratio . What does 1 kg cost?  , Ksh : the mean of the two prices.    Why is only the mix costed by the mean of the prices?  With equal masses each price counts equally. With more maize, the cheaper price counts more and pulls the cost down.    At home, make a small mix of your own (water and milk for chai, or maize and beans) and cost 1 litre or 1 kg of it at your local prices.  Open.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise the components and cost of a mixture.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: in every part, the cost of 1 kg was the total cost divided by the total mass.  The components of a mixture are found by sharing in the ratio.  The mean of the prices is right only for equal masses.      Mixtures  A mixture is made of two or more components . When the components are mixed in a ratio, find each component by sharing in that ratio. The cost of 1 kg (or 1 litre) of the mixture is   It always lies between the two prices, nearer the one used more; it is the mean of the prices only when equal masses are used. The cost of a mixture leans towards the ingredient you use more of.    Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Chai for the School   The school kitchen makes of milk and water for chai, in the ratio . Milk costs Ksh a litre (the water is not costed). How much milk is used, and what does 1 litre of the mixture cost?    parts make , so one part is : milk and water .  The milk costs , Ksh , so 1 litre of the mixture costs , Ksh .     A Coffee Blend   A roaster blends of grade AA coffee at Ksh a kilogram with of grade AB coffee at Ksh a kilogram. What does 1 kg of the blend cost?    Total cost: . Total mass: .  , so Ksh a kilogram: nearer the AB price, because more AB is used.     Dairy Meal   A farmer mixes maize germ and sunflower cake in the ratio to make of dairy meal. Maize germ costs Ksh a kilogram and sunflower cake Ksh . Find the mass of each and the cost of 1 kg of the meal.    One part is : maize germ and sunflower cake .  Total cost: , so 1 kg costs , Ksh .     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type numbers only, without Ksh, units, spaces or commas, for example 45326 . Give a fraction as a fraction, for example 7\/12 , or as a decimal correct to 3 decimal places. Give times, masses and volumes exactly or correct to 1 decimal place, and money to the nearest shilling, unless the question says otherwise.            A bag of feed contains maize bran, cotton seed cake and minerals in the ratio . Find the mass of each.  , and    of rice at Ksh a kilogram is mixed with at Ksh . Find the cost of 1 kg of the mixture.  Ksh  .    Uji flour is made of maize, millet and sorghum flour in the ratio , in all. They cost Ksh , Ksh and Ksh a kilogram. Find the mass of each and the cost of 1 kg of the uji flour.  , and ; Ksh  Total cost , and .    A kitchen makes of milk and water in the ratio . Milk costs Ksh a litre. Find the milk used and the cost of 1 litre of the mixture.  ; Ksh to the nearest shilling  Milk costs , and , so Ksh a litre to the nearest shilling.    A farmer blends DAP and CAN fertiliser in the ratio to make . They cost Ksh and Ksh a kilogram. Find the mass of each and the cost of 1 kg of the blend.  and ; Ksh  Total cost , and .    Challenge: Equal Money, Not Equal Mass  Show that mixing equal masses of two foods gives the mean of their prices, but spending equal amounts of money on each does not.  Try Ksh on each of two foods at Ksh and Ksh a kilogram.  Equal masses : . Ksh on each buys and : Ksh for , and , so about Ksh a kilogram, not Ksh .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Activity parts (a) to (c) and . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including .      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
