  "body": " Across the River   A water engineering team measures the River Tana near Garissa. They stretch a rope across the river from bank to bank and measure the depth every . The depths are metres. (The measurements are made up for this activity.)   The cross-section of the river. The depths are measured every ; the dashed straight lines join the measured points.   Cross-section of a river 24 m wide. Depths measured every 4 m are 0, 1.2, 2.0, 2.6, 2.2, 1.4 and 0 m. The river bed is a smooth curve; dashed straight lines join the measured depths and lie above the curved bed.  A horizontal line marks the water surface from 0 to 24 m. Vertical lines go down from the surface at 4, 8, 12, 16 and 20 m, of lengths 1.2, 2.0, 2.6, 2.2 and 1.4 m. The bed is a smooth curve through the bottoms of these lines and the two banks; between each pair of neighbouring points it sags slightly below the dashed straight line joining them.        How many strips do the measurements make, and how wide is each strip?  depths make strips, each wide.    Use the trapezoidal rule to estimate the area of the cross-section.  .    The water flows at . How many cubic metres of water pass the rope each second, and each minute?  Each second a slice of water long passes: each second, and each minute.    The river bed curves smoothly like a bowl between the measured points. Is your estimate of the area too big or too small?  Too small: the straight lines between the measured points lie above the bed, so some water is left out.    How could the team improve the estimate?  Measure the depth more often, for example every : narrower strips follow the bed more closely.      "
