  "number": "6.3",
  "title": "Representation of Vectors",
  "body": " Representation of Vectors       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.2 Vectors    Specific Learning Outcomes  Represent vectors geometrically on Cartesian plane    Grade Level  12    Estimated Duration  40 minutes         Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: a vector is drawn as an arrow; equal vectors have the same length and direction wherever they are drawn; the position vector of a point; end minus start .  Do: draw a vector from a column at any starting point; read a column from an arrow or from two points; decide which arrows are equal.  Apply: find the vectors between places on a village map.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners find that a column vector fixes a length and a direction but not a position.  Teacher's Role Give out grid boards or squared paper with axes from to . After part (b), ask: Are these three arrows the same vector?     Same Vector, Different Places   Draw axes from to on squared paper. Let .     Draw three times: starting at the origin , at and at . Where does each arrow end?  At , and .    What is the same about the three arrows? What is different?  They have the same length and the same direction; only their starting points differ.    shows four arrows: from to , from to , from to and from to . Which of them are equal to ?  and , both . is the reverse; has across and up swapped.    Draw the arrow from to the point and write its column vector.  : the same numbers as the coordinates of .        Four arrows, each labelled at its tail.   Four arrows on a grid: p from (1, −3) to (4, −2), q from (6, 0) to (3, −1), r from (−3, 2) to (−2, 5), s from (5, 2) to (8, 3).      Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise equal vectors, position vectors and the vector between two points.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (b) shows that where an arrow starts does not change the vector.  A position vector starts at , so its column is the coordinates of the point (part (d)).  The vector from one point to another is end minus start .      Equal Vectors and Position Vectors   Equal vectors (also called equivalent vectors) have the same length and the same direction, anywhere on the page. Equal vectors have the same column vector.  The position vector of a point is the vector from the origin to : . Its column is the coordinates of . For example, if is then .  The vector from to is end minus start:     Drag the tip of to change the vector. Drag the tail of the copy anywhere on the grid: it keeps the same column vector, because equal vectors can start anywhere.     Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Drawing from a Column   Draw starting at , and starting at . Where does each arrow end?    From , move left and up: ends at .  From , move right and not up or down: ends at .     Which Vectors Are Equal?   The points are , , , , , , and . Which of , , and are equal?    End minus start for each:   . is the reverse of , and has across and up swapped, so neither is equal to .     Places on a Village Map   On a village map the chief's office is the origin and unit is . The shop is at and the clinic at . Write the position vectors of and , and the vectors and .    and .  is end minus start: , that is west and north. is the reverse: .     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type one number in each box of a column vector: the top box is the move across, the bottom box the move up. Use a minus sign for left or down, for example -3 . Drag each point on the grid; it jumps to the nearest grid point. Every point must be moved before you check.               Vectors in everyday life: a county map gives each place a position from a fixed point. Where would you choose the origin for a map of your village, and why?    Draw these vectors starting at : , and . Give the end point of each.  , ,    Draw starting at and again starting at . Where does each arrow end?  and    is and is . Write and as column vectors.  ,    Find the vectors , , and for , , , , , , and . Which are equal?  , and are equal (all ); .    On a map of a village near Kitui, the chief's camp is the origin and unit is . The dispensary is at and the borehole at . Write the position vectors of and , and the vector . Describe in words.  , ; : west and north.    Challenge: The Fourth Corner  is a parallelogram with , and . Use to find .  Opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal vectors.   . So is across and up from : .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Use the interactive in and activity parts (a) and (b). Work through . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: find the other two points that make a parallelogram with , and . (Answer: and .)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
