  "title": "Multiplication of a Vector by a Scalar",
  "body": " Multiplication of a Vector by a Scalar       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.2 Vectors    Specific Learning Outcomes  Multiply vectors by scalars    Grade Level  12    Estimated Duration  40 minutes         Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know:  multiplies each component by ; positive keeps the direction and negative reverses it; and are parallel.  Do: find and combinations such as ; decide whether a vector is a multiple of and find .  Apply: a velocity at twice the speed in the same direction, or the same speed in the opposite direction.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners draw multiples of a vector and see it stretch, shrink and reverse.  Teacher's Role Give out squared paper. After part (c), ask: What does the minus sign do to the arrow?     Stretching an Arrow   Let .     Draw , , and from different starting points. Write the column of each.  , , .    Compare the length and the direction of each arrow with .  is twice as long in the same direction; is three times as long; is the same length in the opposite direction.    Draw and write its column.  : half as long, same direction.    Juma writes . What did he do wrong?  He added to each component instead of multiplying: .        The vector and the multiples , and .   Vector a = (1, 2) and the vectors 2a, −a and ½a drawn on a grid: 2a twice as long, −a reversed, ½a half as long.      Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise multiplying a vector by a scalar and parallel vectors.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (a) shows that doubles each component; part (d) is the mistake to avoid.  Multiply every component by the scalar.  The sign of the scalar gives the direction; all multiples of are parallel to .      Multiplying by a Scalar  Here a scalar is just a number. To multiply a vector by a scalar , multiply every component by : A scalar multiplies every component; it never adds to them.  If , points the same way as and is times as long. If , points the opposite way and is times as long. In particular is the reverse of . Vectors that are multiples of each other are parallel .  Combinations such as are found by multiplying first and then adding. Subtracting a vector is adding its reverse: .    Drag the tip of and move the slider. The thick dashed arrow stretches, shrinks and reverses along the line of .     Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Multiples of a Vector   . Find , and .    Multiply every component:   and point the same way as ; points the opposite way.     Combining Two Vectors   and . Find and .        Is It a Multiple of a?   . Which of these are multiples of ? For each multiple, give .  (a) (b) (c) (d) (e)    Divide each component by the matching component of . A multiple gives the same number both times.  (a) and : , same direction.  (b) and : , opposite direction.  (c) but : not equal, so not a multiple of .  (d) and : , opposite direction.  (e) and : , same direction.     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type one number in each box of a column vector: the top box is the move across, the bottom box the move up. Use a minus sign for left or down, for example -3 .           Vectors in everyday life: a weather forecast may say the wind will blow from the same direction tomorrow but twice as strongly. Which scalar multiplies today's wind velocity?    . Find , and .  , ,    and . Find , and .  , ,    . Which of these are multiples of ? Give for each multiple: , , , .  ; ; not a multiple ( but );    A cyclist rides with velocity m\/s (east and north parts). (a) What is her velocity if she rides twice as fast in the same direction? (b) What is her velocity if she rides back the opposite way at the first speed?  (a) m\/s (b) m\/s    . Find .     Challenge: Two Unknown Scalars  Find and such that .  Compare the top numbers, then the bottom numbers.  ,  Top: . Bottom: . From the first, ; then , so and . Check: .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Give a multiply each box card. Focus on activity parts (a) to (c) and . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: write as a combination of and . (Answer: , ; not every answer is a whole number.)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
