  "id": "sec-magnitude-of-a-vector",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-magnitude-of-a-vector.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "6.6",
  "title": "Magnitude of a Vector",
  "body": " Magnitude of a Vector       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.2 Vectors    Specific Learning Outcomes  Determine the magnitude of a vector using cartesian coordinates    Grade Level  12    Estimated Duration  40 minutes         Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know:  by Pythagoras; the distance between two points is the magnitude of the vector between them; a square is never negative.  Do: find the magnitude of a column vector and of the vector between two points; find the straight-line distance of a route.  Apply: how far a walker is from the start; the size of a resultant push.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners measure drawn vectors and match the lengths with Pythagoras' theorem.  Teacher's Role Give out squared paper and rulers. Groups of three: one draws, one measures, one calculates; everyone checks the others' work.     Measure, Then Calculate      Draw , and with square for . Measure the length of each arrow.  , and about .    Each arrow, with its across move and its up move, makes a right-angled triangle. Use Pythagoras' theorem to calculate each length.  ; ; .    Does the minus sign in change the length?  No: , the same as .    Mark and . Write and find its length.  ; length .        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise the magnitude of a vector and the distance between two points.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: the measured lengths in part (a) match the calculations in part (b).  Square, add, square root. Give exact answers, or decimals to decimal places.  The distance between two points is the magnitude of the vector between them (part (d)).      Magnitude  The magnitude of a vector (also called its modulus) is its length. It is written . By Pythagoras on the across and up moves,   The distance between points and is . A multiple has magnitude .    The vector and its components.   The vector (4, −7) with a dashed 4 across and 7 down making a right angle; the vector's length is √65.      Drag the tip of the vector. The dashed lines are its components; Pythagoras gives its length.     Magnitude in a Spreadsheet  Put the -components in column A and the -components in column B, starting in row . In cell C2 type =SQRT(A2^2+B2^2) and copy it down the column: each row gives the magnitude of one vector.    Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Magnitude from Components   Find the magnitudes of and ( ).        Distance Between Two Places   On a map with unit to , a borehole is at and a water point at . How far is the water point from the borehole in a straight line?    End minus start: .  .     How Far from the Start?   (a) A learner walks east and then north. How far is she from the start?  (b) The boda boda rider in finished at km from the stage after riding . How far is the rider from the stage in a straight line?    (a) Her displacement is , so the distance is .  (b) , much less than the ridden.     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type one number in each box of a column vector: the top box is the move across, the bottom box the move up. Use a minus sign for left or down, for example -3 . Type a square root as sqrt(73) , or give the decimal to 2 decimal places.               Vectors in everyday life: a phone's map shows both the distance by road and the straight-line distance to a place. Which one is the magnitude of a displacement?    Find the magnitude of each vector: , , , .  ; ; ;    Find the distance for and , and the distance for and .  ;    For the matatu in , find the straight-line distance from the start to the finish. Compare it with the driven.  , less than half the distance driven.    Two learners push a handcart with forces N and N. Find the resultant force and its size. Why is the size not N?  N, of size N. The pushes are at right angles, so their sizes do not simply add.    The vector has magnitude . What can be? Give both values.  or  , so and or .    Challenge: Magnitudes of Multiples  . Without using Pythagoras, find and .  Write each vector as a multiple of .  ;  , so its magnitude is . , so its magnitude is .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Give a square, add, square root table. Work through and . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: find all the vectors with whole-number components and magnitude . (Answer: twelve vectors: , , and , taking every choice of signs.)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
