  "id": "sec-distance-and-speed",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-distance-and-speed.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "7.3",
  "title": "Distance and Speed",
  "body": " Distance and Speed       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.3 Navigation    Specific Learning Outcomes  Determine speed in knots and in kilometres per hour    Grade Level  12    Estimated Duration  40 minutes         Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: a knot is a speed of one nautical mile per hour; speed is distance divided by time; knot is .  Do: find speeds, times and new positions for journeys along meridians, along the equator and along parallels; convert between knots and kilometres per hour.  Apply: ferries, cargo ships and flights in East Africa and across the Indian Ocean.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners find a ship's speed in knots and in kilometres per hour from the positions of two ports and the times of leaving and arriving.  Teacher's Role Before part (b), remind learners that minutes are sixtieths of an hour: , not . Introduce the word knot only in part (d).     The Ship to Dar es Salaam   A passenger ship sails due south from Mombasa to Dar es Salaam. Take both ports to be on the meridian , with Mombasa at latitude and Dar es Salaam at latitude . The ship leaves Mombasa at 18:00 on Monday and arrives in Dar es Salaam at 07:45 on Tuesday.     How far does the ship sail, in nautical miles?  Both latitudes are south, so subtract: . Along a meridian is nautical mile, so the ship sails .    How long does the voyage take? Give your answer in hours as a decimal.  From 18:00 on Monday to midnight is ; midnight to 07:45 is . Total .    Find the ship's average speed in nautical miles per hour.  nautical miles per hour.    Sailors call a speed of nautical mile per hour a knot . One nautical mile is about . Find the ship's speed in kilometres per hour.  (to 1 decimal place). The ship's speed is .        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise the knot, the speed formula and the conversions learners used in the activity.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (c) gave the speed in nautical miles per hour: that is the speed in knots.  Change hours and minutes to hours by dividing the minutes by . Change a decimal part of an hour back to minutes by multiplying by .  Knots to kilometres per hour: multiply by . Kilometres per hour to knots: divide by .      Speed in Knots  A knot is a speed of one nautical mile per hour. Ships and aircraft give their speeds in knots because their charts measure distance in nautical miles.   with the distance in nautical miles, the time in hours and the speed in knots.  Since , a speed of knot is . Write a time in hours as a decimal before dividing: .    Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       A Flight Along the Equator   An aircraft flies due west along the equator from longitude to longitude . The flight takes . Find its speed in knots and in kilometres per hour.    Both longitudes are east, so the difference in longitude is . The equator is a great circle, so the distance is . The time is .     Sailing East Along a Parallel   A cargo ship leaves Mombasa, taken to be at , and sails due east along the parallel at for hours. Find its new position, to the nearest minute.    Step 1: distance.  .  Step 2: change in longitude. Along the parallel , one degree of longitude is long. So to the nearest minute ( ).  Step 3: new longitude. Sailing east increases an east longitude: . The new position is .     From Kilometres per Hour to Knots   An airliner cruises at . Find its speed in knots, and the time it takes to fly , to the nearest minute.    . Time . The decimal part is minutes, so the flight takes .     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type decimals with a point, for example 36.8 . Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign. Choose N, S, E or W from each list.  Give times on the 24-hour clock: type the hours and the minutes in separate boxes, for example 14 and 05 for 14:05.                A ship sails along the meridian from latitude to latitude in days hours. Find its speed in knots and in kilometres per hour.  ;  Opposite sides of the equator, so add: , so . Time . Speed  .    Convert to kilometres per hour.   One knot is an hour, and . So , which is .    A plane flies along the parallel from longitude to longitude at . How long does the flight take?  hours  Difference in longitude . Distance . Time .    A ship sails along the parallel from longitude to longitude in hours. Find its speed in knots and in kilometres per hour, to 1 decimal place.  ;  Difference in longitude . Distance . Speed ; .    Challenge: A New Position  A ship at sails due west along its parallel at for hours. Find its new position, to the nearest minute.  Find the distance first, then divide by the length of one degree of longitude on this parallel.   Distance . Change in longitude . Sailing west decreases an east longitude: .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Work through the activity and . Give a card: h min to hours: divide the minutes by 60 . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: two ships leave the same point on the parallel , one due east at and one due west at . After hours, how far apart are they along the parallel, and what is their difference in longitude? (Answer: ; .)     Closing question for the class: where in Kenya is speed measured in knots? (Ships at Mombasa and Lamu, aircraft at Jomo Kenyatta and Wilson airports.)   Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
