  "number": "8.2.2",
  "title": "Simple and Compound Interest Compared",
  "body": " Simple and Compound Interest Compared  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: simple and compound interest at the same rate give the same amount after one year; after that compound pulls ahead, and the gap widens.  Do: compare offers at different rates; find, by trial, the first whole year in which a target is passed.  Apply: choose a savings offer.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners use the interactive to compare simple and compound interest on the same savings and explain why the gap widens.  Teacher's Role Pairs record the gap after , , and years before discussing part (c).     Watching the Gap Grow   Ksh is saved for years at per annum. Use or a calculator.     Find the amount after years with simple interest and with compound interest.  Simple: . Compound: to the nearest shilling.    Find the gap between the two amounts after , , and years.  Ksh , , and (to the nearest shilling).    Why is there no gap after one year?  In the first year both pay interest on the principal only. Compound interest starts to earn more only when interest has been added to the account.    Move the rate slider to . Does compound interest still pull ahead?  Yes, more slowly: the gap after years is about Ksh .        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To compare the two kinds of interest and to decide between savings offers.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: the gap in part (b) was after one year and grew every year after that.  Simple interest adds a fixed sum each year; compound interest multiplies by a fixed number each year.  A higher simple rate can beat a lower compound rate over a short time; over a long time compound wins.      Which Grows Faster?  With simple interest the amount grows by the same number of shillings every year, so its graph against time is a straight line. With compound interest the amount is multiplied by the same number every year, so its graph is a curve that gets steeper. At the same rate the two are equal after one year; after that compound interest is always ahead, and the gap keeps growing.    Simple and compound interest on the same savings. The dashed line is simple interest and the solid curve is compound interest.     Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       SACCO or Bank?   Mumbi has Ksh to save. Her SACCO offers per annum simple interest; a bank offers per annum compound interest. Which is better over years, over years and over years?    The SACCO adds a year; the bank multiplies by a year.   Amounts in Ksh   Years SACCO, simple Bank, compound Better  2 SACCO  3 bank, by  6 bank    Over years the SACCO's higher rate wins. From years on, the bank's compound interest is ahead, and the gap grows.     How Many Years?   A chama saves Ksh at per annum compound interest. After how many whole years is the amount first more than Ksh ?    Try values of in : The amount passes Ksh during the sixth year, so it is first more than Ksh after whole years.     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type amounts as plain numbers, without Ksh, spaces or commas, for example 45326 . Give money to the nearest shilling and percentages to 1 decimal place unless the question says otherwise; exact answers in cents are also accepted.            Ksh is saved for years at per annum. Find the amount at simple interest and at compound interest, and the difference, to the nearest shilling.  Simple Ksh ; compound Ksh ; difference Ksh    Ksh can be saved at per annum simple interest or at per annum compound interest. Which is better over years, and which over years?  years: against , simple. years: against , compound.    Ksh for years: is per annum simple interest or per annum compound interest better?  Compound, by about Ksh ( against )    At per annum compound interest, after how many whole years has money first doubled?  years  , just short of ; . So the money has first doubled after years, whatever the principal.    Ksh is saved at per annum compound interest. After how many whole years is it first more than Ksh ?  years  After years: . After years: .    Challenge: Why the Gap Starts in Year Two  Explain why simple and compound interest at the same rate give the same amount after year, and show that after years compound interest is ahead by exactly .  Expand .  Both pay in year 1. In year 2 compound interest also pays on the first year's interest.  After one year both amounts are . After two years, compound gives and simple gives . The difference is : the interest, at , on the first year's interest .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Activity parts (a) to (c) and . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension (digital): build a spreadsheet with a column of simple-interest amounts and a column of compound-interest amounts for Ksh at for years. (Answer: after years, against .)     Closing question for the class: a savings offer pays a high simple rate for one year only. When would you choose it over a lower compound rate?   Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
