  "id": "sec-appreciation",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-appreciation.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "8.3",
  "title": "Appreciation",
  "body": " Appreciation       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.4 Commercial Arithmetic III    Specific Learning Outcomes  Compute appreciation and depreciation in value of assets    Grade Level  12    Estimated Duration  40 minutes         Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: appreciation, the rate of appreciation, and .  Do: find a value after years and the appreciation in Ksh; find the rate over one year, and the equal yearly rate over two years.  Apply: land and houses in Kenyan towns.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners find the yearly rate from a run of land values and use it to predict a later value.  Teacher's Role If learners know real land prices in their area over recent years, use those; otherwise use the values given. Part (d) can be homework.     A Plot Near Kitengela   A family bought a plot near Kitengela in 2021. These are its values in four years (invented for this activity).   Value of the Plot   Year 2021 2022 2023 2024  Value (Ksh)       Find the increase in value each year, in Ksh.  , and .    Write each increase as a percentage of the value at the start of that year.  , and : each year.    If the value keeps rising at the same rate, predict its value in 2026.  2026 is years after 2021: , Ksh .    Ask at home: is land in your area gaining value? Why?  Answers vary: new roads, water, electricity, a growing town or a new school nearby can all raise land values.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To name appreciation and to link it to compound interest.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: the increases in part (a) grew, but the rate in part (b) stayed at , because each increase was worked on the value at the start of the year.  Appreciation works like compound interest: multiply by each year.  A rate is always found from the value at the start of the year.      Appreciation  An asset appreciates when its value goes up over time; the increase in value is its appreciation . If the value rises by of its value at the start of each year, is the rate of appreciation , and the value is multiplied by the same number, , every year. A value becomes, after years, and the appreciation is .  The rate over one year is . Over two years at an equal yearly rate, , so .    The value of an asset that appreciates at a fixed yearly rate.     Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Five Years On   A plot in Nanyuki bought for Ksh appreciates at a year. Find its value after years and the appreciation, to the nearest shilling.    The value is Ksh and the appreciation is , Ksh .     Rate from Two Valuations   A house in Nakuru was valued at Ksh and a year later at Ksh . Find the rate of appreciation.    The increase is , so the rate is Divide by the value at the start of the year, not the new value.     An Equal Rate over Two Years   A plot rises in value from Ksh to Ksh in years, appreciating at the same rate each year. Find the yearly rate.    So : the rate is a year. The total rise is , but halving it to is wrong, because the second year's rise is worked on a bigger value.     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type amounts as plain numbers, without Ksh, spaces or commas, for example 45326 . Give money to the nearest shilling and percentages to 1 decimal place unless the question says otherwise; exact answers in cents are also accepted.            A house worth Ksh appreciates at a year. Find its value after years and the appreciation, to the nearest shilling.  Value Ksh ; appreciation Ksh    A plot bought for Ksh appreciates at a year. Find its value after years, to the nearest shilling.  Ksh    A plot was valued at Ksh and a year later at Ksh . Find the rate of appreciation.     A shamba in Kiambu rose in value from Ksh to Ksh in years at an equal yearly rate. Find the rate.   and , so the rate is a year.    A value rises by a year for years. By what percentage has it risen altogether?  (not )  , so the value has risen by , about .    Challenge: Three Years at an Equal Rate  A plot rose in value from Ksh to Ksh in years at an equal yearly rate. Find the rate.  Use the cube root.   and , so .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Activity parts (a) to (c), and . Give a card: multiply by each year . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: why might land in one county appreciate faster than in another? (Discussion: roads, a growing town, water and electricity, demand from buyers.)     Closing question for the class: is buying land always a good way to save? What could make a plot lose value?   Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
