  "id": "subsec-mean-by-calculation",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "subsec-mean-by-calculation.html",
  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "9.1.1",
  "title": "Mean by Calculation",
  "body": " Mean by Calculation  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: the mean uses every value; a frequency table is a short way of listing repeated values.  Do: find a mean as and as .  Apply: find means of real Kenyan data, and a missing value from a given mean.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners find a mean directly, see that the deviations from the mean add to zero, and (Core) discover that any working value gives the same mean.  Teacher's Role Put learners in groups of 10. Each group measures its heights to the nearest centimetre with a tape fixed to the wall; groups without a tape use the heights in the table. Do not name the assumed mean until after part (d).     Heights in Our Group   A group of 10 Grade 12 learners at a school in Nyeri measured their heights, in centimetres.   Heights of 10 Learners (cm)   162 158 171 165 168 159 174 163 166 170       Find the total of the heights and the mean height.  Total ; mean .    Subtract the mean from each height. Add the ten answers. What do you notice?  ; the sum is . It is always , because the mean balances the data: the amounts above it cancel the amounts below it.    Every learner in the group stands on a block high. Without adding ten new heights, what is the new mean?  . Adding to each of the ten heights adds to the total, and so to the mean.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise the mean of a list and of a frequency table, and (Core) the assumed mean.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: the mean uses every value; that is why one very tall learner moves it.  A frequency table lists repeated values once, with a count: multiply each value by its frequency before adding.      The Mean  The mean of values is their total divided by : where means the sum of . In a frequency table the value occurs times, so every value counts, weighted by its frequency: .  The deviations from the mean, , always add to .    Using a Calculator or Spreadsheet  A scientific calculator in statistics mode finds once you enter the values (with their frequencies, for a table). In a spreadsheet, put the values in cells A1 to A10 and type =AVERAGE(A1:A10) . For a frequency table, put in column A and in column B, then the mean is =SUMPRODUCT(A1:A7,B1:B7)\/SUM(B1:B7) . Always check that the answer lies between the smallest and largest values.    Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Household Sizes   The number of people in each of households in a village near Kitui is recorded below. Find the mean number of people per household.   People per Household   People ( ) 1 2 3 4 5 6 7  Frequency ( ) 3 7 10 12 8 6 4      Multiply each value by its frequency to make an row:   Working for the Mean Household Size   1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Total  3 7 10 12 8 6 4 50  3 14 30 48 40 36 28 199    The mean is people per household. It is not a whole number, even though every household has a whole number of people.     Sacks of Maize   The masses, in kg, of sacks of maize at a cereals depot in Eldoret are . Find the mean mass.    Add the masses: . Then The mean mass is .     A Missing Day's Takings   A boda boda rider's takings over days had a mean of Ksh . On five of the days the takings were Ksh , , , and . Find the takings on the sixth day.    The total over days is . The five known days add to . So the sixth day's takings were , that is Ksh .     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type decimals with a point, for example 30.6 . Give answers to the accuracy asked for.        The 100 m times, in seconds, of six runners at a sports day in Kakamega were . Find the mean time, correct to 1 decimal place.  s  , so s.     The goals scored per match in a county league are shown below. Find the mean number of goals per match, correct to 1 decimal place.   Goals ( ) 0 1 2 3 4 5  Frequency ( ) 6 11 9 5 2 1    goals  and , so goals.     The number of days learners in a class were absent in a term is shown below. The mean is days. Find .   Days absent ( ) 1 2 3 4 5  Frequency ( ) 3 9 6 2     and . So , giving , and .    Challenge: Shifting the Data  Every height in increases by . Find the new mean, and explain your answer using .  Write the new total in terms of the old total , then divide by .   The new mean is . Adding the same number to every value adds that number to the mean.     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Focus on activity parts (a) to (c) and . Provide a column card with headings , , . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: show that for any data (answer: ).      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
