  "id": "subsec-probability-distributions",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "subsec-probability-distributions.html",
  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "10.2.2",
  "title": "Probability Distributions",
  "body": " Probability Distributions  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: a count or score that depends on chance is a discrete random variable ; its probability distribution lists every value with its probability; each probability is from 0 to 1 and they add to 1.  Do: build a distribution from a sample space, find a missing probability, and add rows for .  Apply: customers at a kiosk and goats sold at market.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners build the distribution of the number of heads in two tosses and test it against class data.  Teacher's Role Pairs toss two coins times. After part (b), ask: Why is one head twice as likely as two heads?     Heads in Two Tosses   Two coins are tossed. Let be the number of heads.     List the four outcomes.  HH, HT, TH, TT.    Complete a table of the values and the probability of each.  , , .    Add the probabilities in your table. Why must you get this answer?  : every outcome gives exactly one value of .    Toss two coins times and record each time. Compare your frequencies with .  Expected frequencies , , ; class results should be close to these.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To define a discrete random variable and its probability distribution.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: the table in part (b) is a probability distribution.  Capital names the random variable; small is one of its values.  Two checks: every probability is from 0 to 1; the probabilities add to 1.  Add rows for at least , and remember that at least 2 includes 2.      Probability Distributions  A discrete random variable  is a count or score that depends on chance and whose values can be listed, such as the number of heads in two tosses. Its probability distribution is a table of each value with its probability .  In every probability distribution, each probability is from 0 to 1, and the probabilities of all the values add to 1. To find the probability of several values, add their rows: for example .    A spinner with eight equal sectors, and the distribution of its score shown as a stick graph.   A spinner with eight equal sectors labelled 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, beside a stick graph of the probability of each score: 3\/8 for 1, 1\/4 for 2, 1\/4 for 3 and 1\/8 for 4.  Left: a circle divided into eight equal sectors labelled 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3 and 4, with an arrow. Right: vertical lines above x = 1, 2, 3, 4 with heights 3\/8, 1\/4, 1\/4 and 1\/8.      Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Score on a Spinner   The spinner in has eight equal sectors labelled 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4. Let be the score. Write down the probability distribution of and find .    Count sectors out of :   Distribution of the Spinner Score   1 2 3 4     Check: . Then .     Customers at an M-Pesa Kiosk   Let be the number of customers who arrive at an M-Pesa kiosk in Kisumu in a 5-minute period.   Customers in 5 Minutes   0 1 2 3 4  0.15 0.3 0.25 0.1    Find (a) , (b) , (c) .    (a) The probabilities add to 1: , so and .  (b) .  (c) , which is also .     Goats Sold on Market Day   A farmer in Narok sells goats on a market day, with , , and . Find , write out the distribution, and find .    , so and . The probabilities are , , and , and .     Number of Sixes with Two Dice   Two dice are rolled. Let be the number of sixes. Find the probability distribution of .    Use the -cell grid. Only the cell (6, 6) has two sixes. The last row and the last column hold cells with at least one six, so cells have exactly one six. The other cells have none.  , , . Check: . A tree diagram, as in , gives the same answers another way.     Probability in daily life: seed companies print a germination rate on each packet, found by testing many seeds, as in . What other estimates from experiments have you seen?   Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type a fraction with a slash, for example 5\/12 , and a decimal with a point, for example 0.35 . Give probabilities exactly: do not round.            A spinner has five equal sectors labelled 1, 2, 2, 3, 3. Write down the probability distribution of the score.  , ,    takes the values 1 to 5 with probabilities , , , and . Find , and .  ; ;  . . .    Which of these are probability distributions? Give a reason for each. A: . B: . C: . D: .  A and D. B adds to ; C has a negative value, although it adds to .    for . Find and write out the distribution.  ;  , so . The probabilities are .    Three coins are tossed. Find the probability distribution of the number of heads, and .  for ;    Challenge: A Fun-Day Stall (Extension)  At a school fun-day stall you pay Ksh to spin the spinner in and receive Ksh times your score. Find the average amount paid out per spin in the long run. Does the stall make money?  Imagine spins. About how many of each score would there be?  Ksh per spin, so the stall loses Ksh a spin on average.  In spins expect about ones, twos, threes and fours. The total score is , an average of per spin. The stall pays out shillings a spin on average but takes only Ksh . (The average score is called the expected value of .)     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Give table frames with a total column. Focus on and . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: change the prize so that the stall makes Ksh a spin on average. (Ksh times the score pays out shillings a spin on average.)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
